WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.200
So when I was taught as a student, I was taught

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:06.520
by a chemist at Cambridge school Alfie Maddock.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.800
Who during the Second World War was working in Canada

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:15.260
on Plutonium chemistry. And one night he managed

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.320
to spill the entire UK's  supply of Plutonium.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.800
Which was only 10 milligrams onto the bench in the lab.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.760
And so rather then saying 'oh dear I have spilt it',

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.320
he went and got a saw and sawed a large chunk out of

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.760
the bench, and burned the wood, and got back 9.5 of the 10 Mg

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.920
that he spilled. And nobody would have known

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.960
except at that point, he was too tired to repair

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.600
the bench. So they came down  in the morning, and there was

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.840
a large hole in the bench, to show what he had done.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.480
Ok so Plutonium. I think Plutonium is quite well known

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.840
for the fact its been used in certain types of nuclear bombs.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.960
It's a fairly interesting element, it's incredibly dense.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.800
If you hold a golf ball size shape of Plutonium in your hand,

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.240
it would weigh in excess of half a kilogram.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.200
Which is one heavy golf ball. And if you were holding it,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.960
you would have to be holding it through several layers of gloves,

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.210
at least one of which would be lead lined.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.600
And as you held it, it would be like having a really hot cup of tea

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.800
in your hand. And that's from the radioactivity coming out.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.280
Just the shear heat of the radioactivity which you 

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.920
would be feeling. I also had an interesting experience

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:41.600
with... At a conference, I was sitting next to a lady, who I asked her

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.790
why she was at the conference, which was about 

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.160
super critical fluids. High pressure gases, solvents.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.800
And I asked her why she was there and she said she worked

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.720
with weapons grade Plutonium. What is used in making

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.880
Atom Bombs.  And she said she was worried

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:05.110
about the environmental effects of using chlorinated solvents.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.800
There are compounds of Carbon and Chlorine, for removing the greace

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.960
from the Plutonium that we are using to make the bombs.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.400
So she was at the conference to see whether you could use

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.840
high pressure Carbon Dioxide or as an alternative to

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.800
make the making of atomic bombs environmentally friendly.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.280
That was slightly macabre. Worrying about the environment.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.000
I like that, wanting to make more environmentaly friendly bombs.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.520
I have not seen any because the sort of facilities you need to be

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.800
able to handle it are way beyond what you find 

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.640
in a normal university. I've had some fascinating stories

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.120
about it, it's incredibly dense. And a few people at the Atomic

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.840
Weapons Agency wanted to cut a little piece off,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.600
it took three of them with a  hacksaw several hours,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.800
and several hacksaw blades, before they got a tiny little

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.800
chip off. So it's incredibly dense and hard. Hence the high

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.000
weight for its size. 

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:11.400
Plutonium is quite a poisonous element, and there have been

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.960
a number of cases. Particularly the famous case in America,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.800
with Karen Silkwood, who was poisoned, or had traces of poisonous Plutonium

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.720
found in her refrigerator, and all through her flat.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.120
Which in fact inspired my brother to write a play called

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.400
"Stronger Then The Sun" in the early 1970s. One of his first successful

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.000
television films. Perhaps a little known thing

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.280
about Plutonium, 'cause obviously it has a pretty bad image

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.640
in terms of its use in  nuclear weapons, is that it is what

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.840
helped power the astronauts on the moon landings

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.400
in the 60s and 70s. 

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.900
